  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nTonight through Friday Night...\n\nSkies are partly sunny this afternoon, with thin high clouds \nbeginning to increase in coverage from the west. A mid-level \nshortwave trough over Missouri will lift northeast across Illinois \ntonight, bringing us a period of mostly cloudy conditions this \nevening and tonight. These clouds will exit off to the east \nWednesday morning, allowing for a mostly sunny Wednesday. \n\nOvernight temperatures will remain a bit milder than recent nights \ndue to the increased cloud cover, which will also limit fog \nformation. Temperatures are forecast to dip into the upper 40s and \nlower 50s. Wednesday starts off with strong sfc high pressure \ncentered over New England. The high shifts southeast out over the \nAtlantic, and light southerly flow develops across the Ohio Valley. \nExpect a warm, pleasant Wednesday with highs in the mid to upper \n70s. \n\nWeak shortwave ridging develops aloft to finish out the work week. \nHigh pressure at the surface remains in control across much of the \nMid-South and Mid-Atlantic, though a deepening low pressure system \nover Canada will attempt to drag a cold front through the Ohio \nValley. However, this moisture-starved boundary eventually stalls \nout. Thursday and Friday will remain dry and pleasant, with morning \nlows in the 40s to around 50 and afternoon highs in the mid/upper \n70s. \n\n\nSaturday through Monday Night...\n\nExpect a dry, but cloudier start to Halloween. Clouds will increase \nheading into the weekend ahead of upper level energy associated with \nthe remnants of Tropical Storm Vance (E. Pacific). We'll see steady \nsouthwesterly moisture advection Saturday into Sunday as the upper \nlevel wave lifts northeast over the Mid-MS Valley. LREF mean PW \nvalues are forecast to creep above 1.25 inches by Sunday morning. \nSome modest timing differences linger amongst LREF members and \ndeterministic global model guidance. At this time, we have medium-\nhigh confidence (70-80%) in mainly dry conditions through sunset on \nHalloween. However, the chance for light rain does gradually \nincrease Saturday evening and especially overnight. The bulk of the \nlight rain is expected Saturday night and Sunday. As for amounts, \nLREF median QPF currently suggests 0.25-0.50 inches of rainfall is \npossible through the end of the weekend. \n\nBeyond Sunday, confidence in the details is much lower. However, a \ngenerally active, wetter pattern is expected during the early to \nmiddle portion of next week.",
